\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 A few observations can be made from this 
\f3\fs26 TradingState
\f1\fs24 's properties:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The algorithm\'92s buy orders for 
\f3\fs26 \'93PRODUCT1"
\f1\fs24  matched first with the full quantity of the sell order at price 11. As a result the order is now gone from the order_depths and a corresponding own_trade is created.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The remaining order quantity of 3 then matched with part of the order at price 12, resulting in a second trade at price 12. As can be seen in the order_depths the quantity of the corresponding sell order is now reduced by 3 as well.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 For \'93PRODUCT2\'94 a trade at price 143 with a quantity 2 can be observed, which indicates that one of the bots decided to send a buy order of quantity 2 as a reaction to the player\'92s sell order at that price level. For the player\'92s initial order this means that a quantity of 3 of the 5 total remains unexecuted. None of the bots decides to trade against this order, and the full order quantity is automatically cancelled.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Technical Notes\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 There are a few technicalities that players should take into account when writing an algorithms:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Only the libraries noted at the bottom of this page under \'93Supported Libraries\'94 are allowed to be used by the algorithm. These are listed in Appendix C.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Each time the \'93run\'94 method is called, it should generate a response in 900ms, which should be reasonable as the average is \uc0\u8804  100ms; otherwise, the function call will time out. Players should make sure that their algorithms are sufficiently lightweight to make sure this requirement is met.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec2 Available Resources to Help Build the Algorithm\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 To aid players in building the algorithm several resources are made available:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 For every new product introduced several days of sample data are provided. For each of these days two .csv\'92s are available, one containing a list of all the trades done on that day, and one showing the market orders at every time step. Examples of the file formats:\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl1\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 .csv file with trade example\
\ls11\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 .csv file with market orders example\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 When players upload their algorithms on the Prosperity platform, the algorithm is tested for 1000 iterations using data from a sample day (different than the actual day that will be used for the challenge). After the run a log file is provided which can aid players in debugging their algorithms. To aid debugging, the log file also contains the output of any print statements that players put within the 
\f3\fs26 run
